+    "expected_old_sha256": null,
+    "lesson": {
+      "id": "SUP-HOME-CHILD-SAFETY",
+      "gapIds": [
+        "WG-HE-SL549"
+      ],
+      "subjectName": "Home Economics",
+      "termLabel": "2nd Term",
+      "title": "Child care (continued): safe care in diarrhoea, constipation and colic, safe infant feeding, and immunisation",
+      "origin": "New teaching material prepared under the owner's 15 October 2026 authorisation to use internet equivalents; not recovered school-source text. It supersedes, in delivery, the original Second-Term row 'CHILD CARE [CONTD]', which gave a homemade salt-and-sugar rehydration recipe, advised adding glucose, sugar or honey to a child's food for constipation, and printed a vaccine table that listed smallpox. The original row is preserved unchanged in the audit record and must not be used as study text, fallback or question source. This is school teaching, not medical advice for a particular child; it gives no dose and no homemade medicine recipe.",
+      "sources": [
+        {
+          "title": "Clinical management of acute diarrhoea — WHO/UNICEF joint statement",
+          "url": "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/WHO_FCH_CAH_04.7"
+        },
+        {
+          "title": "Diarrhoeal disease — WHO fact sheet",
+          "url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/diarrhoeal-disease"
+        },
+        {
+          "title": "Foods and Drinks to Avoid or Limit — CDC",
+          "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/infant-toddler-nutrition/foods-and-drinks/foods-and-drinks-to-avoid-or-limit.html"
+        },
+        {
+          "title": "How to Clean, Sanitize, and Store Infant Feeding Items — CDC",
+          "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/hygiene/about/clean-sanitize-store-infant-feeding-items.html"
+        },
+        {
+          "title": "Nigeria routine immunisation schedule — NPHCDA Immunization FHIR IG",
+          "url": "https://build.fhir.org/ig/Nigeria-FHIR-Community/NPHCDA-ImmunizationIG/health-interventions-recommendations.html"
+        }
+      ],
+      "body": "Objectives: describe diarrhoea, constipation and colic in babies and say what a caregiver should do; explain why old home remedies are unsafe; state what a baby under 12 months must not be given; explain why immunisation matters and why families follow the current clinic schedule.\n\nWhy this lesson corrects the earlier notes: the earlier notes on child care (continued) included a homemade salt-and-sugar drink, advice to add glucose, sugar or honey to a child's food for constipation, and a vaccine table that listed smallpox. Those items are unsafe or out of date. Do not use them. This lesson replaces them.\n\nTeaching:\n1. Diarrhoea means three or more loose or liquid stools in a day. The danger is dehydration (loss of too much body water), which is most dangerous in babies. Causes include germs in unsafe food or water, unclean feeding equipment and unwashed hands.\n2. What to do for diarrhoea: keep feeding and keep breastfeeding; give extra fluids; use oral rehydration salts (ORS) from a clinic, pharmacy or community health worker, mixed exactly as the packet says with clean water. Do not mix a salt-and-sugar drink by guessing amounts: too much salt or sugar can make a baby worse. A health worker may also give zinc for the days the child needs it. Do not give anti-diarrhoea medicines, antibiotics or herbal mixtures unless a health worker prescribes them.\n3. Go to a clinic at once if the child is a young baby, is very sleepy or cannot drink, has sunken eyes, passes blood in the stool, has a high fever, vomits everything, or passes very little urine. Diarrhoea that continues needs a health worker.\n4. Preventing diarrhoea: wash hands with soap and clean water after the toilet, after changing a nappy and before feeding; use safe drinking water; keep food covered; clean feeding equipment as in the baby-care lesson (wash with soap and a brush kept for the purpose, sanitise, air-dry and store covered); follow the clinic immunisation card.\n5. Constipation means hard, painful or infrequent stools. Do not add honey, sugar or glucose to a baby's food or drink to treat it. Honey must never be given to a child under 12 months: it can cause infant botulism, a serious food poisoning. Children under 12 months should not be given fruit or vegetable juice, and children under 24 months should avoid added sugars. Take a baby to a health worker for constipation. For older children, plenty of safe water, vegetables, fruit and whole foods and active play help; if there is severe pain, vomiting, a swollen belly or blood, go to the clinic.\n6. Colic means a baby cries hard and long, often in the first months, for no clear reason. Comfort the baby by holding, rocking and a calm room. Take the baby to a health worker if there is fever, vomiting, blood in the stool, poor feeding or the baby seems very unwell. Do not give herbal mixtures or medicines unless a health worker advises.\n7. Safe infant feeding rules: breast milk is the best food for a young baby; a health worker advises when other foods begin (complementary foods begin around six months). Under 12 months do not give honey, cow's milk as a main drink or juice. Avoid salty foods and added sugar. Use clean cups, spoons and bottles. Cook foods well and give them fresh.\n8. Immunisation protects children and expectant mothers from serious diseases. Smallpox was eradicated worldwide, so smallpox vaccine is not part of the routine schedule and the old table must not be used. Nigeria's routine schedule is set by the National Primary Health Care Development Agency (NPHCDA) and can change, so families follow the immunisation card given at the primary health centre. Diseases the routine programme protects against include tuberculosis, polio, diphtheria, tetanus, whooping cough, hepatitis B, pneumonia, measles, yellow fever and meningitis. Keep the card safe, take it to each visit, and ask the health worker which vaccine is due.\n9. Laundering baby clothes (from the original notes): wash and rinse thoroughly so that no soap or stain irritates the skin, and dry in clean air.\n\nWorked example: Tola's baby brother has watery stools. Tola washes her hands, tells her mother, and they keep breastfeeding and take him to the health centre. The nurse gives ORS and explains how to mix it exactly as the packet says. Tola does not give honey, a salt-and-sugar drink or herbal medicine, because she knows these are unsafe.\n\nPractice (questions):\n1. Define diarrhoea and say why it is dangerous for babies.\n2. State three things a caregiver should do when a baby has diarrhoea.\n3. Why must a home salt-and-sugar drink not be mixed by guessing?\n4. List three danger signs that mean the child must go to the clinic at once.\n5. Why must honey never be given to a baby under 12 months?\n6. State two safe ways to help a baby with colic and one sign that needs a health worker.\n7. Why must the old vaccine table that listed smallpox not be used? Where do families find the current schedule?\n8. Name three ways of preventing diarrhoea.\n\nAnswers (marking guide): 1. Three or more loose or liquid stools in a day; the baby can lose too much body water (dehydration) quickly (2). 2. Any three (3): keep feeding and breastfeeding, give extra fluids, give ORS mixed as the packet says, wash hands, go to a health worker, give zinc only if the health worker advises. 3. Wrong amounts of salt or sugar can harm the baby (1). 4. Any three (3): very sleepy or cannot drink, sunken eyes, blood in the stool, high fever, vomits everything, very little urine, a young baby. 5. It can cause infant botulism, a serious food poisoning (1). 6. Hold, rock, calm room (2); fever, vomiting, blood in the stool or poor feeding needs a health worker (1). 7. Smallpox has been eradicated and the schedule has changed (1); the immunisation card and the primary health centre, set by NPHCDA (1). 8. Any three (3): hand washing, safe water, clean feeding equipment, covered food, immunisation. Teacher: this lesson gives no dose and no homemade medicine; do not add any."
+    }
