+      "title": "Electrical energy: circuits, meters and electricity bills (corrected formulas and units)",
+      "origin": "New teaching material prepared under the owner's 15 October 2026 authorisation to use internet equivalents; not recovered school-source text. It supersedes, in delivery, the original Second-Term row 'Electrical Energy.', which describes the voltmeter as an instrument to detect the flow of current, writes the parallel-resistance rule as 1/RI+1/R2 for total resistance R, and prints a meter example whose cost per unit (1291 naira) does not match the total shown. The original row is preserved unchanged in the audit record.",
+      "sources": [
+        {
+          "title": "Electric circuits chapter summary — Siyavula Physical Sciences",
+          "url": "https://www.siyavula.com/read/za/physical-sciences/grade-10/electric-circuits/17-electric-circuits-06"
+        },
+        {
+          "title": "Resistors in circuits — physics.info",
+          "url": "https://physics.info/circuits-r/summary.shtml"
+        }
+      ],
+      "body": "Objectives: use series and parallel rules correctly; name what each meter measures and how it is connected; use V = I x R; calculate an electricity bill from units used; state basic electrical safety.\n\nTeaching:\n1. Current is the flow of charge, measured in amperes (A) with an ammeter connected in series. Potential difference (voltage) is measured in volts (V) with a voltmeter connected in parallel across the part being measured. A galvanometer detects a very small current. Resistance is measured in ohms.\n2. Direction: conventional current is drawn from the positive terminal round the circuit to the negative terminal. Electrons actually move the opposite way.\n3. Ohm's law: V = I x R, so I = V / R and R = V / I.\n4. Series circuit: one path; the same current in every part; total resistance R = R1 + R2 + R3. If one lamp fails, all go off.\n5. Parallel circuit: separate branches; the same voltage across every branch; the currents add. Total resistance follows 1/R = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3. Adding a branch lowers the total resistance. Houses are wired in parallel so that one lamp failing does not switch the others off.\n6. Fuse and circuit breaker: both stop the current when it is too large. A fuse melts; a circuit breaker switches off and can be reset. Fit the correct rating.\n7. Electricity bill: energy used is shown in units (kilowatt-hours). Units used = present reading minus previous reading. Cost = units used x cost per unit.\n8. Safety: never touch bare wires, never use wet hands on switches or sockets, do not overload sockets, and have repairs done by a qualified electrician.\n\nWorked example: Two resistors of 4 ohm and 4 ohm are joined in parallel: 1/R = 1/4 + 1/4 = 2/4, so R = 2 ohm. In series they would total 8 ohm. A meter reads 47896 before and 48167 now: units used = 48167 - 47896 = 271. At 12.91 naira per unit the cost is 271 x 12.91 = 3498.61 naira.\n\nPractice (questions):\n1. Which meter measures current and how is it connected? Which measures potential difference and how is it connected?\n2. Write the formula for total resistance in series and in parallel.\n3. Find the total resistance of 6 ohm and 3 ohm in parallel.\n4. A 12 V battery drives a current of 2 A through a resistor. Find the resistance.\n5. A meter reads 1500 then 1620. The cost is 15 naira per unit. Find the cost.\n6. Why are houses wired in parallel?\n\nAnswers (marking guide): 1. Ammeter, in series (2); voltmeter, in parallel (2). 2. Series R = R1 + R2 (1); parallel 1/R = 1/R1 + 1/R2 (1). 3. 1/R = 1/6 + 1/3 = 3/6, so R = 2 ohm (2). 4. R = V / I = 12 / 2 = 6 ohm (2). 5. 1620 - 1500 = 120 units; 120 x 15 = 1800 naira (2). 6. One failed lamp does not switch off the others; each appliance gets the full voltage (2)."
