+      "body": "Objectives: describe the physical and emotional changes of puberty in boys and girls; explain menstruation and hygiene; state the consequences of teenage pregnancy; name safe sources of help.\n\nTeaching:\n1. Puberty is the stage when a child's body changes into an adult body. It begins at different ages for different people, and being earlier or later than friends is normal. Hormones cause the changes: in girls mainly oestrogen, in boys mainly testosterone.\n2. Changes in both: a growth spurt, hair under the arms and in the pubic area, more sweat and body odour, oily skin and pimples, and strong feelings and mood changes. Wash daily and wear clean clothes.\n3. Changes in boys: the voice deepens (it may crack for a while), shoulders broaden, the testes and penis grow, facial hair begins, and erections and wet dreams happen. Wet dreams are normal.\n4. Changes in girls: breasts develop, hips widen, and menstruation (monthly periods) begins. Periods may be irregular at first. Use clean pads or other safe sanitary materials, change them regularly and wash. Cramps are common; see a health worker if pain is severe or bleeding is very heavy.\n5. Pregnancy can happen once ovulation starts, which can be before or soon after a girl's first period, and it can happen from the first time a person has sex. Not having sex is the surest way to avoid pregnancy and STIs.\n6. Consequences of teenage pregnancy: higher health risks for a young mother and baby, so ante-natal care at a clinic is important; interrupted schooling; loss of plans and income; emotional stress; and risk from unsafe abortion, which can cause severe bleeding, infection, damage to the womb and death. Any girl with bleeding, severe pain or fever after a pregnancy loss or an attempted abortion must go to a health facility at once.\n7. Where to get help: a parent or trusted adult, the school counsellor or teacher, and the nearest health centre. Asking early is wise and safe.\n8. Resist pressure: say no clearly, leave the situation, and tell a trusted adult.\n\nWorked example: Dayo's voice cracks and he feels embarrassed. His teacher explains that this is a normal change of puberty and that it settles with time.\n\nPractice (questions):\n1. Name the hormones mainly responsible for puberty in girls and in boys.\n2. State three changes that happen in both sexes.\n3. State two changes specific to boys and two specific to girls.\n4. How should a girl manage her periods hygienically?\n5. State three consequences of teenage pregnancy.\n6. Name three people or places where a young person can get help.\n\nAnswers (marking guide): 1. Oestrogen; testosterone (2). 2. Any three (3): growth spurt, body hair, sweat or odour, pimples, mood changes. 3. Boys: voice deepens, testes and penis grow, facial hair (any two, 2); girls: breasts develop, hips widen, menstruation (any two, 2). 4. Clean sanitary materials, change regularly, wash (2). 5. Any three (3): health risks, interrupted schooling, loss of plans, emotional stress, unsafe abortion. 6. Parent or trusted adult, school counsellor, health centre (3)."
