+        {
+          "title": "Menarche (first period) and pregnancy myths — HealthLink BC",
+          "url": "https://www.healthlinkbc.ca/healthwise/menarche"
+        }
+      ],
+      "body": "Objectives: define teenage pregnancy; state its causes and consequences; separate myths from facts about pregnancy; state the care needed in pregnancy.\n\nTeaching:\n1. Teenage pregnancy is pregnancy in a girl aged 10 to 19, married or not.\n2. Causes include lack of accurate information, early marriage and social pressure, poverty, use of alcohol or drugs that lead to unwanted sex, and not using protection correctly. No method other than not having sex is completely certain.\n3. Consequences: health risks such as high blood pressure and heavy bleeding; anaemia; unsafe abortion; interrupted schooling; loss of plans and income; and emotional stress.\n4. Myths and facts. Fact: a missed period may signal pregnancy but can have other causes. Fact: pregnancy lasts about nine months (around 40 weeks), not over a year. Fact: a girl can become pregnant the first time she has sex. Fact: pregnancy can occur during or soon after a period. Fact: washing after sex does not prevent pregnancy. Myth: every pregnant woman vomits. Myth: pregnant women should not bathe or are unclean. Fact: pregnant women should not drink alcohol or smoke and should take only medicines a health worker approves.\n5. Care in pregnancy: a clinic confirms pregnancy and gives ante-natal care; eat nourishing food including iron-rich foods as advised; rest; take only prescribed medicine; plan to give birth with a skilled attendant at a health facility.\n6. Danger signs needing urgent help at any time: vaginal bleeding, severe headache or blurred vision, fits, fever, severe abdominal pain, or much less baby movement.\n7. If a girl suspects pregnancy: tell a trusted adult and go to a health facility early. Do not take herbs or drugs, or go to an unqualified person, to end a pregnancy: unsafe abortion kills. The law on abortion is strict; the teacher or school counsellor explains it. A girl with bleeding, pain or fever after a pregnancy loss must go to a health facility at once.\n\nWorked example: Hauwa misses her period. She talks to her aunt, who goes with her to the clinic. A test shows she is pregnant and the nurse arranges ante-natal care.\n\nPractice (questions):\n1. Define teenage pregnancy.\n2. State three causes and three consequences of teenage pregnancy.\n3. Say whether each is a myth or a fact: (a) pregnancy lasts about nine months; (b) a girl cannot become pregnant the first time she has sex; (c) every pregnant woman vomits.\n4. State four things a pregnant woman should do for her health.\n5. Name three danger signs in pregnancy.\n6. Why must a girl never use herbs or an unqualified person to end a pregnancy?\n\nAnswers (marking guide): 1. Pregnancy in a girl aged 10 to 19 (1). 2. Three causes (3) and three consequences (3) from the lists above. 3. (a) Fact (1); (b) Myth, because she can (1); (c) Myth (1). 4. Any four (4): attend ante-natal care, eat nourishing food, rest, avoid alcohol and smoking, take only prescribed medicine. 5. Any three (3): bleeding, severe headache or blurred vision, fits, fever, severe pain, reduced baby movement. 6. Unsafe abortion can cause severe bleeding, infection and death (2)."
